porchetta is a super salty fatty  boneless pork roast stuffed with tons of  garlic herbs and lemon it's so good  hi I'm Matthew Francis I'm a tasty  producer and I'm going to show you how  to make classic porchetta from the tasty  ultimate cookbook first you're going to  want to prep your pork belly a full day  before you cook the porchetta now I know  this seemed like a ton of sugar and salt  but trust me don't freak out you need  this much of the seasoning to flavor all  of this meat and after it rests for a  full 24 hours in the fridge you rinse  off all the seasoning before it's ready  to be cooked this process it's called  dry brining dry brining the pork belly  for a full 24 hours really allows those  flavor enhancers to draw out moisture  and soak into the meat growing up only  my dad or my grandpa could make the  porchetta and they were watched like a  hawk by my super Italian great-grandma  Lulu I was never allowed to even touch  it but then when I came back for  Christmas after my freshman year of  crawlin Airy school I challenged my dad  that I could do a great job just like  him then eventually he gave it the whole  family loved it they loved it but now  because of all that I have to make it  every single year this garlic herb and  lemon paste is so flavorful as the  porchetta slowly cooks in the oven it  perfumes all of that meat and I can't  even describe it it's incredible  I moved far away from home to start  working in restaurants when I was 18 and  the only time I'm able to come home is  for Christmas so cooking food for my  family is my way of showing them how  much they mean to me I love those bozos  more than anything so now a fresh pork  loin is wrapped up in this pork belly it  allows the final product to have both  that super crispy skin you need on the  outside and perfect tender juicy pork in  the center it is so important to tie  your porchetta correctly I lay all the  long strings of twine on the bottom  place the porchetta on top and then tie  the meat by drawing the strings over  creating a loop with two fingers to  create a slip knot tighten that as much  as you can then create a second knot to  hold  place this step is vital to making sure  your pork cook fully evenly and looks  desirable when you try and wow your  family at a dinner table cut off the  excess string and you're good to go if  you tie up perfectly even porchetta it's  just gonna make the end product so  pretty so now the porchetta is ready to  be cooked low and slow in the oven do  this about maybe four hours before your  guests arrive that way you're not  stressed or in a rush when they show up  then you take it out get rid of some of  that fat that will melt off and then you  put it back in the oven at a higher  temperature for a short time to get that  perfect crispy skin this process is  similar to reverse searing reverse  searing is a perfect trick to cook  expensive meats that you're scared to  mess up because you don't want it to be  wrong inside or burnt on the outside  since porchetta has a thick layer of fat  and skin the intense dry heat breaks  open the fat cells and puffs the skin up  and that's how you get the beautiful  crackling and then by the time your  family arrives and they're hungry the  porchetta should be rested and ready to  eat this bad boy is a showstopper for  sure everyone is gonna be like oh my god  I can't believe you made that but you  can do it it's not that hard I know you  can my family we sort of just eyeball  everything so I'm just so excited we  have the classic recipe in our new  cookbook tasty ultimate it's good  because my family will now stop arguing  about the minor details and just let me  make it in peace if you do get the book  and make this bruschetta  let me know because trust me you're  never gonna want to go back to life  without it it's that good  the skin is so crispy it crunches the  garlic and the herbs perfume the whole  entire house and the center of that pork  just melts in your mouth  every Christmas I just have to have  it's perfect oh yes
 

